y we can get  that molecular  real-time data and figuring out what  reagents and methods  has really that support that goal has  really been an  important component to the development  of our esp  i want to acknowledge members of the esp  team and that's in the upper picture  as well as members of the esp long range  auv team and that's the lower picture  some of the work that i also presented  was work that we did with the university  of hawaii including um  with edelong and dave carl and then our  collaborators from noaa greg ducette  reagan era  steve ruberg paul daniel and kelly  goodwin  and the majority of this work was really  is was funded by the david and lucille  packer foundation the betty and  gordon moore foundation and a few nsf  grants  to chris schulman thank you very much  for listening and i'll take your  questions now  there you go i can't see anything  and thank you christina for that  informative presentation and we'll start  the  live q a portion of the webinar now so  if you have any questions you'd like to  ask please do so now  just click on that ask a question box  located on the far  left of your screen we'll answer as many  questions as we have time for  so let's take a look at our questions  already coming in  casino our first question is  what is the salt concentration of the  concentrated  sample and do you have downstream issues  with qpcrs if so  do you make any additions to your pcr  mixes to improve the simplification  well thanks susie for that question um  whenever we try and sample we're  concentrating seawater which has a  salinity of about 35  parts per thousand but  the particles are getting concentrated  not the salt on the filter and so one  of the methods that we utilize to get  rid of the  the sample the sample itself is we  evacuate  all the sea water out and so when we add  the buffer we have less than  um between somewhere between 100 to 50  microliters of residual seawater left so  it's  it's not making a big impact on our  sample sometimes we do have  inhibition that comes in with the sample  and we've overcome that by just doing a  simple dilution so usually  a one to five uh or a one to ten  dilution  is enough for us to get past the  inhibition and that usually has to do  more with the organisms themselves than  the seawater  and how can we ensure that the sampling  process itself  does not disrupt the marine environment  yes that's a great question um ambari we  sit too  at the in a marine sanctuary so we are  very um we are much more restricted with  what we can  do in the bay and we're a lot more um  careful here  everything that we deploy we have to  recover so all the equipment we have to  bring back includes recovering anchors  that are that are we utilize  to hold our instruments in place we also  don't um release any chemicals so  anything that we  um what we do release is the filtered  seawater  but we don't release any other chemicals  whenever we  are sampling we have waste containers  inside of our instruments that hold all  of that  material so it's really the structure  that might  impact organisms within the marine  environment but we  again after we're done sampling we  remove that from the environment  thank you christian i want to thank our  audience these are great questions  coming in we have quite a bit we'll try  to get it to as many as we can  this next one is a couple of part um  two-part questions  what are the advantages of conducting  research  in laboratories at sea that's the first  part  and what kind of underwater research can  produce results more effectively  than research that can be done on land  in particular what kind of research can  be done  under water that are not directly  related to marine life  okay those are really great questions um  uh first of all  it has to do with when you really want  that information for  for us if you're thinking about  monitoring or even like  a management situation or you want to  find an environment  where you can target organisms that  you're really interested in  it's nice to be able to have that  information before you start  sampling rather than after you you go to  all this really big effort  um in terms of uh things that are more  amendable  obviously the less steps um and things  that are easy to automate  are are much simpler things that take a  lot of  processing or a lot of steps or require  a lot of  informatics to interpret results are a  little bit  harder so we found that things that are  much  faster almost like a point of care where  they give you  an answer that you can then react to  have been  the best there are  definitely methods that are not amenable  to  to working underwater or at sea and in  those cases things like where we can  archive samples or  in the case where um the enzyme  is able to whatever we lice or the  homogeneity that we get back if that's  stable and we're able to analyze that  further when it gets back to shore  those are all things that are are really  important to supporting this kind of  research  and that's been kind of a key um  to us developing this instrumentation is  to know what we bring back  into the lab with the instrument or what  we do at sea actually matches  what normal laboratory methods  have provided over the years  and then the last question was the  organisms that aren't sorry that aren't  directly related to um marine life  um this type of technology um you can  also utilize to monitor for human health  uh targets so there's um obviously  large people a large groups that  manage human pathogens  that they assess water quality and they  close or  shut down beaches and this type of  research or  instrument can actually be utilized to  give more information  in a faster more real time and so it  would just be  working towards qualifying this  particular type of instrument for that  kind of  management resource and so it's really  about  trying to provide information about the  environment  in in real time  thank you christina this next audience  member  has a question about contamination does  working in  oceans increase the chance of  contamination  um it definitely does in that one of the  pictures that we had showed earlier  there was a lot of of bio fouling so it  really depends on where you're at so  in ambari sometimes we operate during  phytoplankton blooms or in coastal areas  and those instruments  do get biofiled we try to  utilize like copper screens and things  like that that might minimize  different types of contamination we also  have ways that we can  internally clean our tubes to try and um  our sampling tubes in order to  keep down different types of biofouling  but that's definitely an issue in the  environment um  and we try and look at targets too that  may or may not come in on that  particular bio  that particular um uh like  sorry we try to target organisms that  aren't necessarily associated with  biofouling  but that's um yeah one way that we do  that  thank you and how long do robotic  samplers laugh  that's a great question so a lot of  times we  we are um at the mercy of how much power  we have and so  like the mobile device that particular  one  will last about three weeks out at sea  um the mooring that we showed you that  one  has operated on its own battery pack for  about  six months and then we've also operated  there's a cabled observatory that's um  that's present in the monterey bay and  we've been able to plug in that and we  were  plugged in and underwater at 800 meters  for about eight months  so a lot of times it really has to do  with your power  it also has to do with how many samples  you want to take during  that particular period so if you want to  try and take a sample a day and you have  in our carousel for the um for the  the mobile device there's 60 samples so  there you're really limited to say  60 days if you could if you could be out  that long and so it really depends  on how frequently you want to sample  where you want to be  how often your phenomena occurs so those  are all  questions that whenever we whenever we  lay out a deployment those are all  things that we have to answer  thank you christina and again i want to  thank our live audience for their  participation  great questions coming in any questions  not answered today will be answered via  emails from our presenter  our next question could you talk a  little bit about  how this remote sampling and analysis  has impacted your resource  allocations i'm wondering about savings  in terms of  transporting the water samples reduced  lab plastics or  others well you definitely um  get uh really good samples it impacts  because you don't have a human  that has to go out and collect those  samples and filter them and then bring  them back to the lab  the added benefit of those lysing  cartridges is that all of that lysis is  done  while the um well the vehicle is out and  so you're getting homogenous back that  can be directly analyzed so it's really  just then recovering that  off of the cartridge our cartridges  right now  are we reuse them and so we clean them  um and then reuse them again and so  there's less plastic waste  in that in in that realm and then we're  not using as many  tubes and things like that and that's  been really the  the biggest benefit of that particular  cartridge and and that method so it's  not just the real time but it's also  the improvement on processing whenever  you get back to the lab  thank you for that this next question  actually piques my interest too  has anyone thought of using this in the  testing of coded  deploying to non-urban areas for testing  um you you certainly could it is a water  sampler it can be utilized  and it can take on any type of water it  would really be  right now we don't have the downstream  pcr requirements instrument in order to  do that analytical  um provide that information in in real  time  but that's definitely something that the  instrument can do those are targets that  it can handle  um and if um  if that were something that somebody  wanted to try it would be  possible now christina is all your  molecular research limited to pcr  amplification studies or do they  progress to  amplicon sequencing of say the 16 sr dna  to understand community representation  so we definitely want to try and move  into sequencing we've already started  one of the other groups that we're  collaborating with at ambari  has started to utilize um the lysates  that we generate on those lysate  on those cartridges to see how they  represent how different um  groups represent they're mostly  targeting a larger  zooplankton so going after more edna  products and so that's definitely a vein  that we're going down  a lot of the initial analysis that we  did  was to utilize qpcr because it's much a  quicker turnaround  and it's easier for us to identify  issues  and since a lot of the some of the  sequencing technology relies on  a targeted pcr approach  if we can get the pcr to work and it  looks and it's good enough and it's  representative of what we normally see  in the lab  we feel like it's a a good transition  to get to sequencing  thank you very much now how often do you  analyze  the maroon water how often do i  excuse me how often do you analyze  the marine of water so  a lot of times what we're doing in the  lab is a lot of development  but we try and um we try and do a number  of deployments per year  in the monterey bay we have between two  and three big expeditions where we  target  particular events so we've done things  like studied  uh daily migration of zooplankton  and looked at that acoustically and then  targeted samples in  in high acoustic layers we have an  upcoming deployment where we're going to  look at canyon dynamics and so the  distribution of different types of  fish and and bacterioplankton and  harmful algal blooms around the bay  um we also generally do one to two  deployments  outside of our our area 